			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468596">
									

<p>I'm no-voting for now, despite liking this overall, because I have two big complaints. I offer this comment because I'm hoping to see what is in my opinion a good skip become a great one.</p>
<p>I'll start with the positive: the scope and length here are more than enough to make this a good SCP-5000, in my opinion. I like that it's an obvious interpretation of the theme but one that goes whole hog. It's essentially a short detective novella set within the SCP Foundation. You genuinely deserve a lot of credit for pulling that off and making it compelling.</p>
<p>My main points of complaint are this:</p>
<p>-I really do not think the rape subplot with our unnumbered SCP who can walk through walls adds anything at all. Rape as a narrative device is something to be used with caution and I don't think simple shock value (which it strikes me as here) justifies its inclusion. I really think you can write that out and make the whole thing a lot more digestible.</p>
<p>-I want to back up Taffeta here:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In re: the ending, I don't particularly care for the Pataphysics reveal because you know what, not every piece of genre work on the site needs a cheeky 'eh? eh? eh?' to justify its existence. I think I can suspend my disbelief and accept that this is a little ''''''unrealistic'''''' when it's this well-written already. I don't really care to have an in-universe explanation that feels somewhat like an attempt to ward off joyless nitpickers moreso than a coherent twist.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But also escalate it. Emma's ability will already leave readers in doubt as to whether Lowry *really* acted of his own volition. Pointing that out at the end once is fine&#8212;and very much in-character for the Foundation. Going farther and involving the Pataphysics Dept. just strikes me as serious overkill, it's kind of the same fundamental problem as ending a story with someone waking up from sleeping. This is just a more involved iteration of that. You need to trust your fiction to stand on its own two legs, and if &quot;joyless nitpickers&quot; as Taffeta colorfully calls them want to complain about it, that is their business.</p>

							</div>

								
					
