			<div class="content" id="post-content-4470512">
									

<p>Thanks for your comments, planet Jane, and I'm pleased that you liked it overall. I'll appreciate the detailed points you make, and I've responded below.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I really do not think the rape subplot &#8230; adds anything at all &#8230; I don't think simple shock value (which it strikes me as here) justifies its inclusion. I really think you can write that out and make the whole thing a lot more digestible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You make the fair point that this may be a failure of imagination on my part. I wanted the SCP to be a threat (because it justifies the Site Director's use as a tool of revenge, and also because the SCP doesn't get much screentime, so needs to be an obvious threat), and I wanted the nature of that threat to be something other than murder (because I wanted variety among my characters, and the D-Class murderer was already there). Choosing sexual assault was probably a cheap decision in that respect, and it felt like it fit the &quot;walking through walls&quot; anomaly in terms of invasion of space.</p>
<p>I'm open to discuss another way of providing the SCP with the same level of threat, and I'll think about it further myself. I don't know that I can remove them entirely without making major changes to the story, as the Site Director and doctor's plots depend indirectly on this SCP's role in proceedings. But let me know any thoughts.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Going farther and involving the Pataphysics Dept. just strikes me as serious overkill, it's kind of the same fundamental problem as ending a story with someone waking up from sleeping.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As noted to Taffeta above, I agree with this, and I may well decide to nix this ending. But I do have some affection for it, in part for the very reason you identify - this is a huge anticlimax. I like that fact, as a playful subversion of expectations. My first two endings are a typical murder mystery ending, followed by a typical Foundation story ending. The Pataphysics ending is exactly the thing that SCP-5000 herself warned against - it's effectively &quot;the aliens committing the crime&quot;.</p>
<p>The other reason I like this ending is that I think it adds to the contest theme, and adds some fridge logic to the piece. While &quot;Pataphysics did it&quot; resolves one mystery, it begs more questions (as SCP-5000 also alluded to) - exactly how and when did they substitute the clone, and how could the events as we've seen them play out with a clone in the containment chamber?</p>
<p>I may yet change the ending (and perhaps beef up O'Connor's note in compensation), but I wanted to highlight why I put it there in the first place, just for discussion purposes.</p>
</div>
</div>
