					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/junkmail-lt" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3093408); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3093408&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795028" alt="junkmail-lt" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3093408)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/junkmail-lt" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3093408); return false;" >junkmail-lt</a></span> <span class="odate time_1579041376 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Jan 2020 22:36</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468801">
									

<p>O'Connor's voice as the protagonist of a mystery novel is really strong here, and the mystery is satisfying and fun.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, the sting kind of undercuts it! It takes away from O'Connor's conclusions as the finale, and makes her &quot;my will is not my own&quot; comments less important since pataphysics is going &quot;of course not lol&quot; and it still just kind of feels out of place. I think this is a stronger piece without it. If you think something needs to go there, I'd suggest a denouement on what happened to all the rest of the characters: what happened with Simpson being outed as a Serpent's Hand operative? What happened to doctor Yau after it was revealed she was sleeping with an anomaly? What happened after it was revealed that May and Grossenbacher were fucking with security?</p>
<p>I'm upvoting, and I like this one a lot - it does justice to the genre, but I can't help but feel that it shoots itself in the foot at the end.</p>

							</div>

								
					
